A Windows program that looks up its own computer name gets an address, as on
Windows, instead of WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND.

Windows always resolves its own host name. Wine hands a program the short
name (GetComputerNameEx with ComputerNamePhysicalDnsHostname, e.g.
"MacBook-Pro" for the host "MacBook-Pro.local"), and on a Mac that short name
resolves only where the network's DNS happens to know it: macOS itself knows
it as "<name>.local", through mDNS. When the lookup fails, getaddrinfo falls
back to a NULL node, and its comment says to avoid a NULL service too because
that is invalid, but it passes the caller's service through. A caller that
gives none, as Python's socket.gethostbyname does, then gets EAI_NONAME, that
is WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND (11001). A PyInstaller-built LAN launcher stopped at
start with "socket.gaierror: [Errno 11001] getaddrinfo failed", right after
Wine's "Failed to resolve your host name IP", on a network whose DNS did not
know the Mac's name; on the network of the day before it had started (engine
engine-wine-11.0-20261030T202616Z-primer-build, macOS 27).

The fallback now tries the fully qualified name first, which on a Mac gives
the machine's addresses, loopback and LAN, through mDNS, and only then the
NULL node, with the service "0" when the caller gave none, as the comment
meant.

Checked 2026-11-10 on macOS 27, with Python's resolver and a C program that
runs the same sequence on the host's getaddrinfo, on a network whose DNS did
not know the short name: the short name and the NULL node with a NULL service
both fail with EAI_NONAME, "<name>.local" gives 127.0.0.1 and the LAN address,
and the NULL node with "0" gives 127.0.0.1. Upstream Wine 11.0 has the same
code; not sent.

diff -ruN a/dlls/ws2_32/protocol.c b/dlls/ws2_32/protocol.c
--- a/dlls/ws2_32/protocol.c	2026-11-10 14:00:00
+++ b/dlls/ws2_32/protocol.c	2026-11-10 14:00:00
@@ -205,16 +205,22 @@
         }
         if (!strcmp( fqdn, node ) || (!strncmp( fqdn, node, strlen( node ) ) && fqdn[strlen( node )] == '.'))
         {
-            /* If it didn't work it means the host name IP is not in /etc/hosts, try again
-             * by sending a NULL host and avoid sending a NULL servname too because that
-             * is invalid */
-            ERR_(winediag)( "Failed to resolve your host name IP\n" );
-            ret = do_getaddrinfo( NULL, service, hints, info );
-            if (!ret && hints && (hints->ai_flags & AI_CANONNAME) && *info && !(*info)->ai_canonname)
+            /* Windows always resolves its own name. A Mac knows the short name only
+             * as "<name>.local", through mDNS: try the fully qualified name first. */
+            if (strcmp( fqdn, node )) ret = do_getaddrinfo( fqdn, service, hints, info );
+            if (ret)
             {
-                freeaddrinfo( *info );
-                *info = NULL;
-                return EAI_NONAME;
+                /* If it didn't work it means the host name IP is not in /etc/hosts, try again
+                 * by sending a NULL host and avoid sending a NULL servname too because that
+                 * is invalid */
+                ERR_(winediag)( "Failed to resolve your host name IP\n" );
+                ret = do_getaddrinfo( NULL, service ? service : "0", hints, info );
+                if (!ret && hints && (hints->ai_flags & AI_CANONNAME) && *info && !(*info)->ai_canonname)
+                {
+                    freeaddrinfo( *info );
+                    *info = NULL;
+                    return EAI_NONAME;
+                }
             }
         }
     }
